Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: realtek: honour realtek,hsgmii on RTL8221B SerDes

The FiberHome AN4475-G2 wires the RTL8221B to the Airoha EN7581 ETH PCS and
describes the link as phy-mode = "2500base-x".  Mainline then programs
MMD 30.0x697a[5:0] = 2, i.e. standard 2500BASE-X (64B/66B), while the EN7581
PCS driver selects AIROHA_SCU_ETH_XSI_HSGMII and sets AIROHA_PCS_TBI_10B_MODE
for PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX, i.e. it speaks Realtek HiSGMII (8B/10B).
It never enables standard 2500BASE-X.

The two ends therefore speak different line codes on the same 3.125G lane:
the PHY's copper side links normally, but the host PCS receives nothing.

  pcs_pma DIG_RO_RESERVE_2 RX_SIGDET = 0/6     (PON PCS, same method: 6/6)

Datasheet 8.6.1 defines 30.0x697a[5:0] as: 0 = 2500Base-X+SGMII,
1 = HiSGMII+SGMII, 2 = 2500Base-X, 3 = HiSGMII.  Mainline has no HiSGMII
constant at all, so a board in this situation cannot express the mode it
needs through phy-mode alone.

Add the missing mode and honour an explicit realtek,hsgmii devicetree
property, the same way other Realtek-based boards do.  Boards that do not
set the property keep the existing behaviour.

HiSGMII keeps the lane at a fixed 3.125G and only carries 2.5G
(datasheet 7.20.2), so a copper link negotiated at 1G needs the same pause
based rate matching that 2500BASE-X already gets - report RATE_MATCH_PAUSE
for this mode in both config_init and get_rate_matching, otherwise the link
comes up but no frame survives.

Signed-off-by: local

--- a/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
@@ -132,6 +132,12 @@
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_MASK		GENMASK(5, 0)
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX_SGMII		0
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX		2
+#define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_HSGMII			3
+
+static int rtl8221b_serdes_mode = -1;
+module_param_named(rtl8221b_serdes_mode, rtl8221b_serdes_mode, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rtl8221b_serdes_mode,
+		 "Force the RTL8221B SerDes option mode 0-3 (-1 = use DT/auto)");
 
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_CTRL3			0x7580
 #define RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_CTRL3_MODE_MASK		GENMASK(5, 0)
@@ -1999,6 +2005,29 @@
 		phydev->rate_matching = RATE_MATCH_NONE;
 	}
 
+	/* Some hosts call their 3.125G port "2500base-x" in DT but run
+	 * Realtek HiSGMII on the wire: Airoha EN7581 selects
+	 * AIROHA_SCU_ETH_XSI_HSGMII for PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX and
+	 * never enables standard 2500BASE-X. Programming mode 2 there
+	 * leaves the host PCS with no received signal at all.
+	 *
+	 * rtl8221b_serdes_mode overrides both DT and the default so the two
+	 * candidates can be A/B tested on a live box:
+	 *   modprobe -r realtek && modprobe realtek rtl8221b_serdes_mode=2
+	 */
+	if (rtl8221b_serdes_mode >= 0) {
+		mode = rtl8221b_serdes_mode;
+		phydev->rate_matching = (mode == RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX ||
+					 mode == RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_HSGMII) ?
+					RATE_MATCH_PAUSE : RATE_MATCH_NONE;
+	} else if (device_property_read_bool(&phydev->mdio.dev, "realtek,hsgmii")) {
+		mode = RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_HSGMII;
+		/* HiSGMII keeps the lane at 3.125G, so a copper link below
+		 * 2.5G still needs pause based rate matching from the MAC.
+		 */
+		phydev->rate_matching = RATE_MATCH_PAUSE;
+	}
+
 	/* the following sequence with magic numbers sets up the SerDes
 	 * option mode
 	 */
@@ -2145,6 +2174,13 @@
 	    RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX)
 		return RATE_MATCH_PAUSE;
 
+	/* HiSGMII also runs the lane at a fixed 3.125G, so it needs the same
+	 * pause based rate matching as 2500BASE-X.
+	 */
+	if ((val & RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_MASK) ==
+	    RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_HSGMII)
+		return RATE_MATCH_PAUSE;
+
 	/* RTL822X_VND1_SERDES_OPTION_MODE_2500BASEX_SGMII */
 	return RATE_MATCH_NONE;
 }